A||||15||DecadenceR||Windshield Swap||||1||
Z||000000||DecadenceR||06-22-2001||02:53 PM||||How hard is it to replace a windshield?<BR>Any tips?<BR>Reccomendations?<BR>Or should I just call Safelite and get them to do it? LOL||||reg||1
Z||000001||filthyscarecrow||06-22-2001||03:56 PM||||yeah, mine got cracked when i spun and hit the curb. i'd be interested in this one too... how much should we expect to pay for a new or a used WS? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-22-2001||04:04 PM||||from what I've seen on previous posts - its glued in<P> if you can get your hands on a used one thats great - but let the pros put it in for ya<P>  (what do they call them guys? Glassers - there is another name for them, Glaiser?!?)||||reg||1
Z||000003||pherder||06-22-2001||04:05 PM||||I called around my area and got a quote to replace my windshield for $135 plus tax.<P>Highest quote was for $209.<P>Since the low quote company guaranteed (in writing) that there would <B>NOT</B> be any optical distortions in the glass, I decided that I would go with them.<P>Walk out price for a windshield was $90 from their wholesaler (plus tax).  Having removed a windshield once before on a Fiero, I wasn't in a hurry to try again, even though I have the correct tool now to cut out the windshield.  Plus I would have to get the proper "glue" to put it back in AND buy and install the trim around the windshield!<P>Was worth it to me to have it done and it took less than an hour to have them do it in their shop.  <P>Also most companies will come to the car and replace the windshield.<P>On another note ... the windshield I needed to replace was cracked from one side to the other <B>AND</B> top to bottom!  While getting a quote for a replacement, I was asked "If you took a dollar bill and put it on the damage, would it cover it?"  I told her, "If I had several hundred dollar bills, I probably could cover the damage but I believe a new windshield would cost less!"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>She laughed and I told her the damage.  Her company ended up being the low quote and I did have them do the replacement.<P>Paul<BR>(New windshield in the 86 SE that was perfectly clear for one day!!!  It muded (rained) the next day.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||06-22-2001||04:25 PM||||thats what I always do with windshields. not worth the effort for that low a price. Simple to just write the check. And most give you a lifetime warranty too.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||06-22-2001||04:37 PM||||BTW - I havent really noticed from photos of Fieros - did they come from the factory with the blue tint across the top 5" of the glass - or can I take it my windsheild has been replaced cause I have that?<P>  I dont mind it on my Fiero - hardly ever notice it - but when the windsheild was replaced on my dodge van, they put one like that in (I wasnt home - wife was -she said it would be ok). Problem with the van was, I am 6' tall and I had to look though the blue tint most of the time, unless i sat hunched over.<P>   if you get yours replaced make sure they dont put one of those in unless you want it that way.||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||06-22-2001||04:37 PM||||I've changed a couple windshields in Fieros. I haven't had any luck removing them without damage though. The lower corners are a ***** , you really have to remove the fenders if you have a good windshield from a junk Fiero you want to salvage. But as far as installing them, it's a ***** , but not beyond ability. You pull out the weather strip, cut the sealant with a special tool, maybe slide some safety wire in and have one hand inside, the other outside the window and break the seal. What you have to look out for is to not cut the headliner, it sits real close. Once you remove the old windshield, clean the mating surface of the vehicle and the windshield, lay some window tape you get from Autozone for $8 in the track, and lay the windshield in. You can usually find a used windshiel for $50, so if you're on a budget, you can do it for $60 and about three hours.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SteveJ||06-22-2001||04:47 PM||||My next door neighbor owns a collision shop and I've had him do a couple for me. He has used the wire to cut the bead but I have also seen him put his foot on the inside of the windshield and push it out in pieces.||||reg||1
Z||000008||V8Dreamer||06-22-2001||04:57 PM||||I'd recommend just getting a new one, they look so nice when freshly installed that you'll definately appreciate the new glass although it costs more.  But then again that's just me......I'm also a poorcollegeboy (one word)  and as of right now would not be buying any windshield, new or used bc I'm saving up to do a 3800 II conv.<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000009||DecadenceR||06-22-2001||05:30 PM||||Thanks for the advice guys.<BR>I think I'm going to wait then and have a new one put in. I know the one in my camaro was only like $250, this shouldn't be too much different. i just figured since i have the extra parts car here, I'd do that. But if it's such a pain, it's not worth the money I'd save and with my luck I'd have to pay for it anyway cause I'd break something.||||reg||1
Z||000010||EdsB52||06-22-2001||07:06 PM||||If you have a parts car w/a windshield, I would pull it and throw it in your good car. Figure about 8 hours and a lot of patience.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||84Bill||06-22-2001||07:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DecadenceR:<BR><B>How hard is it to replace a windshield?<BR>Any tips?<BR>Reccomendations?<BR>Or should I just call Safelite and get them to do it? LOL</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>NO NO NO NO Safelite bites. Well for me they did. If you get someone to do it there is a question you need to ask them. Do you have this? It's a small ribber spacer just about an 1/8 inch that goes between the windshield and the frame. <BR>If they don't install it the windshiled will sit to low in the frame and the trim will look all stupid. Not to mention the windshield will stress crack if it is to close to the frame.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/op/spacer.jpg"> <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||DaRkLoRD||11-12-2001||10:50 PM||||What do you do if the corner of your windshield is loose, and you can actually push the glass up a bit with your hand???<P>is there a quick fix for this (what kind of glue??) until I can get it to Apple Auto Glass etc?<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000013||tgowens||11-13-2001||01:08 AM||||DaRkLoRD, windshields are installed with Polyurethane - you might get a small tube and squeeze some into the corner and press it into place........<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||donalson||11-13-2001||01:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B>On another note ... the windshield I needed to replace was cracked from one side to the other [b]AND</B> top to bottom!  While getting a quote for a replacement, I was asked "If you took a dollar bill and put it on the damage, would it cover it?"  I told her, "If I had several hundred dollar bills, I probably could cover the damage but I believe a new windshield would cost less!"    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>She laughed and I told her the damage.  Her company ended up being the low quote and I did have them do the replacement.<P>Paul<BR>(New windshield in the 86 SE that was perfectly clear for one day!!!  It muded (rained) the next day.)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1st thing i'd do is call your insurance...if you have full coverage (in FL at least) are required to replace your window for free...i'm not sure about other states...it's worth a try.<P>on a side note they asked my wife the same quiestion...she didn't have the witty reply but it wasn't quite that far off haha<P>and another side note...the windshield is a structural part of most cars...you remove the windshield and your car becomes much more flimsy...much like cutting your top off...in a head on collision damage will likely be much worse if you have an improperly installed windshiled...your saftey alone is worth having it done by a pro...and it's for that same reason that most insurance companies will replace your windshild for little to no money.<P>mark<P><P>------------------<BR>'86 mr2 hardtop<P>mid-engine lover||||reg||1
Z||000015||revin||11-13-2001||07:38 AM||||I must say that out of the three fieros at the local junk yard I got none out without cracking them. here is what I learned... Take off the wipers VERY CAREFULLY ! The crack that runs up the glass could come from that. also use piano wire or some other strong wire for "breaking the seal" or cutting through (using a sawing motion)the silicone. A warm day will make it easier! PLEASE go S L O W !<BR>Be sure to get the little stoppers from the bottom of the windshield. I bought the very sticky black silicone from local glass store. I have used the very same thing to mount the roof panel as well as the windshield, and the side window scoops. I might use some to fix a leak in the sun roof. The stuff is hard to get out.Warm it up a little to ease it out better. oh yea...It WILL get everywhere so be carefull!||||reg||1
